<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Persian Gulf War, First Gulf War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf War was the conflict that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s invasion and annexation of Kuwait in August 1990. A coalition of 35 states led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> assembled forces in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and, after an air campaign from January 1991, drove the Iraqi army out of Kuwait in a short ground offensive in February. Iraq remained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> and no-fly zones for over a decade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-war.jpg" alt="Gulf War" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US Air Force · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USAF_F-16A_F-15C_F-15E_Desert_Storm_edit2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Iran-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq-war">Iraq War</a>, the Saudis had lent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> 26 billion dollars, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> felt he should not have to repay it. The talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> ended with an Iraqi demand for 10 billion dollars to cover revenue lost from the Rumaila oil field; Kuwait offered 500 million. Iraq responded by ordering an invasion, which began on 2 August 1990 with the bombing of Kuwait City. The UN Security Council demanded withdrawal on the day of the invasion and authorized a total embargo four days later. On 29 November 1990 Resolution 678 authorized all necessary means if Iraq was not out of Kuwait by 15 January 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The coalition's operation had two phases: Desert Shield, the build-up from August 1990, and Desert Storm, which began with the bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> on 17 January 1991. The air campaign lasted five weeks, with over 100,000 sorties and 88,500 tonnes of bombs, which widely destroyed military and civilian infrastructure. Iraq answered with Scud missile attacks on Israel and then Saudi Arabia, which failed to turn Muslim-majority states against the coalition.</p><p>The ground offensive began on 24 February 1991. The US Marines and other forces crossed into Kuwait, where trenches and minefields were poorly defended and most Iraqi troops surrendered. Meanwhile the US VII Corps attacked into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> just west of Kuwait, and the XVIII Airborne Corps swept across the southern desert, its flank guarded by the French Division Daguet. The 101st Airborne Division made an air assault 249 km behind Iraqi lines, cutting Highway 8 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and the Iraqi forces. Kuwait was liberated on 28 February, and a ceasefire was declared within 100 hours of the ground attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A revolt broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> on 1 March 1991, one day after the ceasefire, and the Iraqi government suppressed a series of uprisings until 5 April, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopters</a> it had been allowed to fly. The coalition countries set up no-fly zones over the north and south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and the UN Special Commission sought to end Iraq's programmes for weapons of mass destruction. Iraqi forces had set over six hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil wells</a> on fire in Kuwait, and over 1,000 Kuwaiti civilians were killed by Iraqis during the occupation. The war was also marked by live news broadcasts from the front, mainly by CNN.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1976 Syrian forces held Lebanon for 29 years, fighting for control through its civil war, and in 1991 Syria joined the American-led coalition in the <b>Gulf War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Iraq fought Iran from 1980 to 1988, conducted the Anfal campaign against the Kurds, including the gas attack on Halabja, and invaded Kuwait in 1990, leading to the <b>Gulf War</b> and sanctions.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-iraq">Republic of Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
